speechtext:
Last November I stated that the government proposed that sugar should be sold at its average laid-down cost, and that will be carried out. As to the views of the beet sugar producers, it might be well if I were to read to the committee a resolution passed by the association and quoted by me in a letter to the secretary of the association on March 3. Here is what the association resolved:
And whereas it is the considered opinion of the directors of this association that quick recovery and normal expansion of this important agricultural industry can only be brought about by return to a completely free sugar economy, without any government buying and selling of raw cane sugar and dictating sales prices of refined sugar;
Now therefore be it resolved to ask the government and all members of parliament to ask the sugar beet districts that the last remnants of sugar control be abolished by March 31, 1948, at the latest, when the emergency powers of the sugar administrator expire;
And be it further resolved to request, if the government is long on raw cane sugar bought, that these quantities be liquidated by the sugar administrator to the cane sugar refineries according to consumption requirements at prevailing market prices including statutory duties, without any further powers of control as to imports, purchase or sales prices, in the same way as any other importer would do.
